        "right": "p0001-n0020-l0006"
      }
    ],
    "section_id": "s0003",
    "source_line_ids": [
      "p0001-n0020-l0000",
      "p0001-n0020-l0001",
      "p0001-n0020-l0002",
      "p0001-n0020-l0003",
      "p0001-n0020-l0004",
      "p0001-n0020-l0005",
      "p0001-n0020-l0006"
    ],
    "source_pages": [
      1
    ],
    "text": "Our models indicate that releasing dragons into the wild could have disastrous ecological impacts, including the elimination of forests, melting of sea ice, and decimation of many prey populations. How- ever, since dragons may provide bene\ufb01ts, such as regulation of nuisance populations and provenance of resources to humans, careful management could turn the ecology of the situation around. Because of the volatile nature of this problem, we devised multiple complementary models to best capture the problem and its potential solution. This approach is of value to population managers dealing with similar environmental concerns with real-world nuisance species and human-caused habitat destruction."
  }
]
